mode gui

// Regression: raylib was unreachable from the INTERPRETER, so `til repl` in
// mode gui ran `InitWindow(800, 600, "hi".c_str)` and opened nothing.
//
// `link("vendor/raylib/src/libraylib.a")` puts a PATH in link_flags, and a
// static archive cannot be dlopen'd. The archive contributed nothing to
// bin/til either -- a linker only pulls in the members the compiler itself
// references, and the compiler never calls raylib -- so dlsym(RTLD_DEFAULT)
// found none of it. Registration then silently skipped every raylib function
// and the calls fell through to the body-less-function path, which returns
// zero: InitWindow "succeeded" with no window, GetRandomValue(5, 5) gave 0.
//
// The interpreter now relinks archives into one shared object with
// --whole-archive and dlopens it (ffi_init_archive_shim). This test is
// INTERPRETED on purpose -- every other gui test builds first, which is
// exactly why the interpreted path stayed broken with a green suite.
//
// It deliberately opens NO window. What regressed is symbol reachability and
// libffi dispatch, not rendering, and every raylib entry point used here is
// pure CPU. The first version called InitWindow and asserted IsWindowReady,
// which made it the only test in the suite that required a working GL
// context -- the compiled gui tests just run for a second and expect
// `timeout` to kill them, so they pass even when no window ever appears.
// That version failed wherever GLFW could not initialize (an X server
// without GLX, or no display at all) for reasons unrelated to the bug it
// guards. Keeping it windowless also keeps leak detection ON: the GLFW
// driver leaks that forced ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0 only happen once a
// window is created.

main := proc() {
    // Plain scalar args and return. Before the fix this silently answered 0,
    // because an unresolved ext symbol fell through to a body-less function.
    SetRandomSeed(12345)
    fixed := GetRandomValue(7, 7)
    if fixed != 7 {
        panic("raylib GetRandomValue did not dispatch through libffi, got: ", fixed.to_str())
    }

    // A struct passed BY VALUE across the bridge, still without a window:
    // ColorToInt packs the four components into 0xRRGGBBAA. Keep the red
    // component under 0x80 so the packed value stays inside I32, which is
    // what the binding returns.
    packed := ColorToInt(Color(r = 17, g = 34, b = 51, a = 68))
    expected : I32 = 0x11223344
    if packed != expected {
        panic("raylib ColorToInt did not receive the Color by value, got: ", packed.to_str())
    }
}
